3. System Architecture and Implementation

3.1 Overall Software Stack

The ENRG-ROBOT-2026-006 stack is architected as a multi-threaded real-time application on a heterogeneous compute platform. The architecture strictly separates safety-critical components (executing on isolated CPU cores with real-time scheduling) from non-critical services.

Compute Platform:

· Primary SoC: NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin 64GB (12-core ARM Cortex-A78AE)
· Real-Time Co-processor: Dual-core Cortex-R5F lockstep MCU for safety monitoring
· Memory: 64GB LPDDR5 with ECC, partitioned into safe/unsafe regions via MPU
· Networking: Dual independent GbE interfaces: PCIe NIC (EtherCAT) + SoC-integrated MAC (DDS)

Thread-to-Core Affinity:

Core Thread Scheduling Priority
0-1 Linux kernel, DDS middleware CFS Normal
2 Risk-Monitor (CVaR) SCHED_FIFO 80
3 Nominal MPC SCHED_FIFO 90
4-5 A-RCBF QP (LLC), EtherCAT master SCHED_FIFO 99
6-7 UKF state estimation, Oustaloup update SCHED_FIFO 95
8-11 Perception, RSSM/WM inference CFS (isolated) Normal

3.2 Real-Time Communication Architecture

3.2.1 EtherCAT/DDS Coexistence

The coexistence of EtherCAT (real-time motion control) and DDS (ROS 2 fleet communication) on the same physical platform presents well-documented challenges. EtherCAT provides deterministic communication at the fieldbus level, while ROS 2 with DDS middleware enables flexible, distributed data exchange at the application layer. However, AES-128-GCM encryption for DDS-SI (Security Interface) generates micro-bursts that can saturate control-plane bandwidth if not properly isolated.

Traffic Isolation Strategy:

1. Physical Isolation: EtherCAT master uses dedicated PCIe NIC with DPDK bypass for zero-copy packet processing; DDS operates on the separate SoC-integrated GbE MAC.
2. Leaky-Bucket Shaping: Control-plane TX rate is capped at 65% of link utilization with burst allowance $<12\%$ to prevent queue head-of-line blocking.
3. Hardware Timestamping: IEEE 1588v2 PTP is deployed on both network stacks. The LLC CBF-QP uses local monotonic clock (CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW) for deadline enforcement, rendering it immune to DDS-induced jitter.
4. Inline QoS: DDS samples are configured with RELIABLE reliability but KEEP_LAST history depth=1 to prevent stale data accumulation.

Latency Budget Verification:

· EtherCAT cycle time: 1 kHz (1 ms)
· Worst-case frame jitter (measured): $<6\ \mu\text{s}$
· DDS AES encryption overhead: confined to fleet telemetry at 10 Hz, never invoked in the real-time control path

3.2.2 A-RCBF QP Implementation Details

The QP solver employs OSQP with the following optimizations for 200 Hz execution:

· Warm-Start: The previous timestep's primal-dual solution initializes the current solve.
· Factorization Caching: The KKT matrix is refactored only when the active constraint set changes.
· Fail-Safe Fallback: If OSQP exceeds its iteration budget (set to 25), the solver returns the best feasible iterate. If infeasibility is detected, a passive backup controller (proportional-derivative with conservative gains) takes over for one cycle.

3.3 Thermal Priming and Safety Integration

3.3.1 Sulfide ASSB Priming Protocol

Sulfide-based all-solid-state batteries (ASSBs) exhibit approximately 30% higher diffusion resistance $R_{\text{diff}}$ below 40°C, which degrades both power capability and state estimation accuracy. Before executing high-C-rate tasks (e.g., lifting payloads $>15$ kg), the High-Level Controller (HLC) triggers a zero-net-torque current rippling sequence:

\tau_j(t) = \Delta \tau \sin(\omega_{\text{rip}} t), \quad \omega_{\text{rip}} \in [60, 120] \text{ Hz}

Differential actuation across joint pairs ensures $\sum_j \tau_j \approx 0$, with residual torque $<0.8$ N·m as verified by harmonic analysis. The heating power is $P_{\text{heat}} = \sum_j I_j^2 R_0$, which raises pack temperature from ambient to $>42^\circ$C within $\leq 90$ s.

ISO 10218-1 Annex B Compliance: The maneuver is classified as "non-hazardous internal heating" provided that (i) net robot motion remains within 2 mm positional tolerance, and (ii) the Stall Detection Function is active. We implement a stall override: if joint torque error exceeds 150% of nominal for $>20$ ms, the priming amplitude is reduced by 50% to avoid exciting structural resonances during hard contact.

3.3.2 Active Soft-Start Contact Wear Mitigation

Fixed pre-charge resistors ($R_{\text{pre}}$) in battery swap connectors cause arc-pitting that accelerates contact degradation. The empirical degradation law under PWM-controlled soft-start is:

R_c(n) = R_{c0} \left(1 + 0.03 n^{0.2}\right)

validated via IEC 60512-11-1 endurance testing. At $n=3000$ cycles, this yields $\Delta P_{\text{loss}} \approx 4.2$ W compared to 12.8 W with fixed $R_{\text{pre}}$. Beyond 3000 cycles, the model transitions to a linear wear regime dominated by fretting corrosion.

