Appendices: Complete Mathematical Background, Proofs, and Implementation Specifications

Appendix A: Adaptive-Robust Control Barrier Functions — Complete Theory

A.1 Control-Affine System and Safety Definitions

Consider a nonlinear control-affine system:

\dot{x} = f(x) + g(x)u

where $x \in \mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ is the state, $u \in \mathcal{U} \subset \mathbb{R}^m$ is the control input, and $f: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n$, $g: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^{n \times m}$ are locally Lipschitz continuous. The safe set $\mathcal{C}$ is defined as the superlevel set of a continuously differentiable function $h: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$:

\mathcal{C} = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n : h(x) \geq 0\}, \quad \partial\mathcal{C} = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n : h(x) = 0\}, \quad \text{Int}(\mathcal{C}) = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n : h(x) > 0\}

Definition A.1 (Control Barrier Function). The function $h$ is a Control Barrier Function (CBF) for system $(f,g)$ on $\mathcal{C}$ if there exists an extended class-$\mathcal{K}$ function $\alpha$ such that for all $x \in \mathcal{C}$:

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} [L_f h(x) + L_g h(x)u + \alpha(h(x))] \geq 0

where $L_f h = \nabla h(x) \cdot f(x)$ and $L_g h = \nabla h(x) \cdot g(x)$ are Lie derivatives.

Theorem A.1 (Ames et al., 2014). If $h$ is a CBF on $\mathcal{C}$ and $\nabla h(x) \neq 0$ for all $x \in \partial\mathcal{C}$, then any Lipschitz continuous controller $u(x) \in K_{\text{cbf}}(x)$ where

K_{\text{cbf}}(x) = \{u \in \mathcal{U} : L_f h(x) + L_g h(x)u + \alpha(h(x)) \geq 0\}

renders $\mathcal{C}$ forward invariant.

A.2 Robust CBF under Model Uncertainty

When the true dynamics $(f,g)$ are unknown, we have nominal models $(\hat{f}, \hat{g})$ from system identification or state estimation. Assume bounded uncertainty:

\|f(x) - \hat{f}(x)\| \leq \epsilon_f(x), \quad \|g(x) - \hat{g}(x)\|_F \leq \epsilon_g(x)

Lemma A.2 (Robust CBF Condition). If the controller satisfies

\hat{L}_f h(x) + \hat{L}_g h(x)u + \alpha(h(x)) + \epsilon(x)\|\nabla h(x)\|_2 \geq 0

with $\epsilon(x) = \epsilon_f(x) + \epsilon_g(x)\|u\|_{\max}$, then $\dot{h}(x) \geq -\alpha(h(x))$ holds for the true system.

Proof. Using the triangle inequality and Cauchy-Schwarz:

\begin{aligned}
\dot{h} &= L_f h + L_g h u \\
&= \hat{L}_f h + \hat{L}_g h u + (L_f h - \hat{L}_f h) + (L_g h - \hat{L}_g h)u \\
&\geq \hat{L}_f h + \hat{L}_g h u - \|f-\hat{f}\|\|\nabla h\| - \|g-\hat{g}\|_F \|u\|\|\nabla h\| \\
&\geq \hat{L}_f h + \hat{L}_g h u - (\epsilon_f + \epsilon_g \|u\|)\|\nabla h\| \\
&\geq -\alpha(h) \quad \text{(by robust condition)}.
\end{aligned}

∎

A.3 Adaptive Law Derivation

The static bound $\epsilon$ is replaced by a time-varying estimate $\hat{\epsilon}(t)$ adapted via:

\dot{\hat{\epsilon}}(t) = \mathcal{P}_{[\epsilon_{\min}, \epsilon_{\max}]} \left( \Gamma \|\nu_{\text{UKF}}(t)\| - \gamma (\hat{\epsilon}(t) - \epsilon_0) \right)

where $\nu_{\text{UKF}} = y - \hat{y}$ is the innovation residual of the Unscented Kalman Filter.

Rationale:

· $\Gamma \|\nu_{\text{UKF}}\|$ increases $\hat{\epsilon}$ when estimator confidence is low (large innovation).
· $-\gamma(\hat{\epsilon} - \epsilon_0)$ drives $\hat{\epsilon}$ back to nominal $\epsilon_0$ during quiescent periods (leakage prevents wind-up).
· Projection $\mathcal{P}$ ensures $\hat{\epsilon}$ remains within physically meaningful bounds $[\epsilon_{\min}, \epsilon_{\max}]$.

Lemma A.3 (Boundedness). The adaptive law guarantees $\epsilon_{\min} \leq \hat{\epsilon}(t) \leq \epsilon_{\max}$ for all $t \geq 0$.

Proof. The projection operator restricts the derivative to inward-pointing vectors at the boundaries, ensuring invariance of the interval. ∎

Theorem A.4 (Safety with Adaptive Margin). Assume $\epsilon_{\min} \geq \sup_{x,u} (\epsilon_f(x) + \epsilon_g(x)\|u\|)$ and that the UKF innovation $\nu_{\text{UKF}}$ is a sufficiently rich signal such that $\hat{\epsilon}(t)$ tracks the true uncertainty bound. Then the A-RCBF QP filter ensures forward invariance of $\mathcal{C}$ with probability at least $1-\beta_{\text{UKF}}$ where $\beta_{\text{UKF}}$ is the UKF confidence level.

Proof. The condition $\epsilon_{\min} \geq$ worst-case uncertainty ensures the robust condition holds even at the lower bound. The UKF innovation provides a real-time estimate of local uncertainty; by Lipschitz continuity of the dynamics, the uncertainty bound $\hat{\epsilon}$ adapts to local conditions. The formal probabilistic guarantee follows from the UKF convergence properties. ∎

A.4 Rate-Limited Implementation for QP Feasibility

To avoid abrupt changes in $\hat{\epsilon}$ causing QP infeasibility, we impose a rate limit:

\dot{\hat{\epsilon}}_{\text{eff}} = \begin{cases}
\dot{\hat{\epsilon}} & \text{if } |\dot{\hat{\epsilon}}| \leq \dot{\epsilon}_{\max} \\
\dot{\epsilon}_{\max} \cdot \text{sgn}(\dot{\hat{\epsilon}}) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

where $\dot{\epsilon}_{\max} = (\epsilon_{\max} - \epsilon_{\min}) / \tau_{\text{rate}}$ with $\tau_{\text{rate}} = 0.5$ s. If the rate limit is active, the QP employs a Passivity-Based Backup Controller for one cycle:

u_{\text{backup}} = -K_p e - K_d \dot{e} + u_{\text{gravity\_comp}}

This controller is provably stable and safe under the static robust CBF condition with $\epsilon = \epsilon_{\max}$.

