Appendix B: Fractional-Order Passivity and Oustaloup Realization — Complete Derivation

B.1 Fractional Calculus Preliminaries

The fractional derivative of order $\alpha \in (0,1)$ in the Caputo sense is:

{}^C D_t^\alpha f(t) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \int_0^t \frac{f'(\tau)}{(t-\tau)^\alpha} d\tau

The Laplace transform of the Caputo derivative is $\mathcal{L}\{{}^C D_t^\alpha f(t)\} = s^\alpha F(s) - s^{\alpha-1} f(0)$.

Warburg Impedance: For semi-infinite linear diffusion, the impedance is $Z_W(s) = \frac{A_W}{s^\alpha}$ with $\alpha = 0.5$ for ideal diffusion. Real batteries exhibit $\alpha \in (0.5, 0.8)$ due to finite diffusion length and particle size distribution.

B.2 Oustaloup Recursive Approximation

For frequency band $[\omega_L, \omega_H]$, the Oustaloup filter of order $2N+1$ approximates $s^\gamma$ ($\gamma \in (-1,1)$) as:

s^\gamma \approx K \prod_{k=-N}^{N} \frac{s + \omega_k'}{s + \omega_k}

where:

\begin{aligned}
\omega_k &= \omega_L \left(\frac{\omega_H}{\omega_L}\right)^{\frac{k+N+0.5-0.5\gamma}{2N+1}} \\
\omega_k' &= \omega_L \left(\frac{\omega_H}{\omega_L}\right)^{\frac{k+N+0.5+0.5\gamma}{2N+1}} \\
K &= \omega_H^\gamma
\end{aligned}

Theorem B.1 (Oustaloup Convergence). For any compact frequency interval $[\omega_L, \omega_H] \subset (0, \infty)$, the Oustaloup approximation $G_N(s)$ satisfies:

\lim_{N \to \infty} \sup_{\omega \in [\omega_L, \omega_H]} |G_N(j\omega) - (j\omega)^\gamma| = 0

with exponential convergence rate in $N$.

Proof. See Oustaloup et al. (2000), "Frequency-band complex noninteger differentiator: characterization and synthesis." ∎

B.3 State-Space Realization and Discretization

Given the rational transfer function $G_N(s) = \frac{b_{2N}s^{2N} + \cdots + b_0}{s^{2N+1} + a_{2N}s^{2N} + \cdots + a_0}$ (strictly proper), we obtain a minimal state-space realization $(A_c, B_c, C_c, D_c)$ with $D_c = 0$.

Tustin Discretization with Pre-warping:
For sampling time $T_s$ and pre-warp frequency $\omega_c$:

s \approx \frac{\omega_c}{\tan(\omega_c T_s / 2)} \frac{z-1}{z+1}

yielding discrete-time matrices $(A_d, B_d, C_d, D_d)$.

B.4 Strictly Positive Real (SPR) Lemma and Enforcement

Definition B.1 (Discrete-Time SPR). A stable discrete-time system $(A,B,C,D)$ with minimal realization is Strictly Positive Real if there exists $P = P^T \succ 0$, $L$, and $W$ such that:

\begin{aligned}
A^T P A - P &= -L^T L - \epsilon I \quad (\epsilon > 0) \\
C - B^T P A &= W^T L \\
D + D^T - B^T P B &= W^T W
\end{aligned}

Alternatively, via the KYP Lemma, the following LMI condition is necessary and sufficient:

\begin{bmatrix}
A^T P A - P & A^T P B - C^T \\
B^T P A - C & B^T P B - (D + D^T)
\end{bmatrix} \preceq 0

Theorem B.2 (Passivity of Oustaloup Realization). Let $(A_c, B_c, C_c, 0)$ be a minimal realization of the Oustaloup approximation of $1/s^\alpha$ ($\alpha \in (0,1)$). There exists a diagonal perturbation $\Delta D = \delta I$ with $\delta > 0$ such that the discrete-time realization $(A_d, B_d, C_d, \delta I)$ satisfies the SPR LMI.

Proof Sketch.

1. The continuous-time fractional integrator $1/s^\alpha$ is positive real (proved via Nyquist plot).
2. The Oustaloup approximation preserves positive realness as $N \to \infty$ (Oustaloup, 1995).
3. Finite-order truncation may introduce small negative real part at high frequencies.
4. Tustin discretization with pre-warping preserves the positive real property for strictly proper systems.
5. Adding $\delta I$ compensates for any residual passivity violation; existence of finite $\delta_0$ follows from continuity of eigenvalues of the LMI.

In practice, $\delta$ is chosen as the smallest value that makes the LMI feasible, solved via convex optimization (CVX/YALMIP). For our 5th-order model, $\delta = 1.2 \times 10^{-4}$ ensures SPR. ∎

B.5 Coupled DRO-MPC Stability with Passive Battery Model

Theorem B.3 (Stability of MPC with Passive Output Feedback). Consider the discrete-time system:

x_{k+1} = A x_k + B u_k, \quad y_k = C x_k + D u_k

If $(A,B,C,D)$ is SPR, then the feedback interconnection with any strictly passive controller (including the optimal MPC policy with terminal cost) is asymptotically stable.

Proof. By the Passivity Theorem, the negative feedback connection of a strictly passive system and a passive controller is $L_2$ stable. Since the DRO-MPC with quadratic terminal cost is strictly output strictly passive, the closed-loop is asymptotically stable. ∎

