D.1 Battery Degradation Model

The instantaneous degradation rate $\dot{Q}_{\text{fade}}$ (in % capacity loss per hour) is:

\dot{Q}_{\text{fade}} = k_1 \cdot (C_{\text{rate}})^{\gamma_1} \cdot e^{\gamma_2 / T} + k_2 \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\text{SOC} > 0.8} \cdot (1 + \gamma_3 \cdot \text{SOH})

Parameters (LFP chemistry, identified from literature):

Parameter Value Source
$k_1$ $2.4 \times 10^{-5}$ Wang et al. (2011)
$\gamma_1$ 1.6 
$\gamma_2$ 3700 K 
$k_2$ $8.1 \times 10^{-6}$ 
$\gamma_3$ 0.5 

D.2 Multi-Class Queue and Assignment Optimization

Let $\mathcal{R}$ be set of robots, $\mathcal{T}$ set of pending tasks. Each task $j$ has:

· Required energy $E_j$
· Expected C-rate profile class $c_j \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$
· Priority $p_j$

Each robot $i$ has state: $\text{SOC}_i, \text{SOH}_i, T_i$, and location.

The SOH-weighted dispatch solves:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{x_{ij}} \quad & \sum_{i \in \mathcal{R}} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{T}} x_{ij} \cdot \underbrace{\dot{Q}_{\text{fade}}^{(i)}(c_j, \text{SOC}_i, T_i, \text{SOH}_i)}_{\text{degradation cost}} \cdot \underbrace{w_{\text{SOH}}(\text{SOH}_i)}_{\text{SOH weight}} \\
& + \sum_{j \in \mathcal{T}} (1 - \sum_i x_{ij}) \cdot p_j \cdot M_{\text{SLA}}
\end{aligned}

subject to:

\begin{aligned}
& \sum_{i} x_{ij} \leq 1, \quad \forall j \in \mathcal{T} \quad \text{(each task assigned at most once)} \\
& \sum_{j} x_{ij} \cdot E_j \leq \text{SOC}_i \cdot Q_{\text{nom}} \cdot \text{SOH}_i, \quad \forall i \\
& \text{SOH}_i \geq \text{SOH}_{\min}(c_j) \cdot x_{ij}, \quad \forall i,j \\
& x_{ij} \in \{0,1\}
\end{aligned}

The weight $w_{\text{SOH}}$ is:

w_{\text{SOH}}(\text{SOH}) = \begin{cases}
1.0 & \text{if SOH} \geq 0.9 \\
1.5 & \text{if } 0.8 \leq \text{SOH} < 0.9 \\
2.5 & \text{if SOH} < 0.8
\end{cases}

D.3 Linearization for MILP Solver

The bilinear term $x_{ij} \cdot \text{SOC}_i$ is handled via big-M reformulation. The degradation cost is pre-computed for each robot-task pair as a constant $d_{ij}$, yielding a pure binary linear program.

Algorithm D.1: SOH-Weighted Dispatcher

```
Input: robots R, tasks T
Precompute d_ij = degradation_rate(robot_i, task_j) * w_SOH(SOH_i) for all i,j
Formulate MILP with objective sum(d_ij * x_ij) + SLA penalties
Solve with branch-and-bound (Gurobi/CPLEX)
Return optimal assignment x_ij
```

